        <OtherAbstract Type="AIDS" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>As HIV and AIDS patients live longer, more neurologic disorders are being reported that require prompt identification and treatment. Neuropathies can be caused by nutritional deficiencies, antiretroviral or chemotherapy drugs, certain toxins such as alcohol, and coexistance of other illnesses such as diabetes. Each of the following neuropathies is discussed: distal painful neuropathy, Guillain-Barre Syndrome, mononeuropathy, cytomegalovirus-related neuropathy, and diabetes. Treatment options are also examined, including practical measures, and results from clinical studies evaluating the efficacy of amitriptyline, mexiletine, acupuncture, and nerve growth factor for neuropathic pain.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
